INT. DRAWBRIDGE CONTROL HOUSE - NIGHT

A glass booth perched high above a river, lit by a desk lamp and a bank of switches. The bridge spans a quarter-mile of black water below. Cars queue on both sides, headlights fogged. A thermos, a paperback, a radio playing low jazz.

HARRIET LOCKWOOD, 50s, a bridge tender in a cardigan, feet on the desk, reads by lamplight. Her hand rests on the control lever.

The door bangs open. A gust of rain.

NATHANIEL BRIGHT, 30s, soaked, panting, a pistol in one hand and a gym bag in the other, kicks the door shut and presses his back to it.

NATHANIEL
Don't move.

HARRIET
(without looking up)
I wasn't going to.

NATHANIEL
Put the book down.

HARRIET
It's a good book.

NATHANIEL
Put it down!

She marks her page with a bus ticket. Sets the paperback on the desk. Raises both hands.

HARRIET
There. Happy?

NATHANIEL
Raise the bridge.

HARRIET
That's a lot of cars.

NATHANIEL
I said raise it!

HARRIET
There's an ambulance in that line, son. Third from the left.

He looks. Through the glass, the pulsing blue of an ambulance in the waiting traffic. His arm wavers.

NATHANIEL
I don't care.

HARRIET
You do.

NATHANIEL
Raise it, or I swear --

HARRIET
You swear what?

He steps forward. The gun shakes. She stays perfectly still, hands up, face calm.

HARRIET (CONT'D)
(to the room, gently)
Everyone's safe up here, young man. It's only a bridge.

NATHANIEL
(to himself)
Don't think. Don't think.

HARRIET
Whoever's behind you, he's not coming for you here.

NATHANIEL
Shut up.

HARRIET
He's not that fast.

Nathaniel lowers the gym bag. Unzips it with one hand. Money, packed tight. He closes it again, hard.

NATHANIEL
Please. I'm asking.

HARRIET
You're not asking. You're aiming.

NATHANIEL
Then what do I do?

She lowers her hands, slow. Reaches for the thermos. Pours a cup of coffee. Holds it out.

HARRIET
You drink this.

NATHANIEL
Are you crazy?

HARRIET
Thirty-one years on this bridge. You're the fourth.

He stares at the cup. The jazz plays on, a soft saxophone. Rain rattles the glass.

NATHANIEL
...What happened to the other three?

HARRIET
One went to jail. One went home. One's the mayor.

He laughs, one broken sound. His arm sinks.

NATHANIEL
I'm not going to jail.

HARRIET
You might.

NATHANIEL
I can't.

HARRIET
Then go home.

Headlights flare across the booth. Sirens, far off, rising.

NATHANIEL
They're coming.

HARRIET
They're always coming.

He takes the coffee. Sets down the gun on the desk beside the paperback. His hands shake so hard the cup rattles on the saucer.

HARRIET (CONT'D)
Sit. You're dripping on my floor.

He sits. She lowers the lever. Far below, the bridge begins to rise, a vast iron hinge turning against the night sky, and every car on both banks goes still.

NATHANIEL
What are you doing?

HARRIET
Giving the river a minute.

The sirens grow. The bridge lifts. Two figures, the tender and the thief, sit side by side in the booth, drinking from the same thermos, listening to the jazz.
